WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.230
Now that Spider-Man has come home to the MCU,
longtime Marvel fans can rest easy.

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:09.360
The new Spider-Man is a hit, and we're sure
to see more of Tom Holland in the red and

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.450
blue webs in the years to come.

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:16.490
But while everyone knows about the importance
of Peter Parker, Aunt May, and Adrian Toomes,

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:20.439
there are lots of other characters hanging
out in this movie who may prove to be much

00:00:20.439 --> 00:00:22.909
more important than you might've suspected.

00:00:22.909 --> 00:00:26.849
Strap in, nerds, and let's chat about the
characters from Spider-Man: Homecoming with

00:00:26.849 --> 00:00:28.269
more meaning than you realized.

00:00:28.269 --> 00:00:30.420
Oh, and do you feel that tingling?

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:37.660
That's your Spidey Sense warning you about
the Spidey Spoilers about to fly at your eyeballs.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:39.180
Michelle

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:43.690
The ending reveal of Michelle's true identity
— that she's "MJ" to her friends" — points

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:47.210
to the coming importance for the character
in Peter Parker's life.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:52.180
As fans of both the comics and the movies
know, Peter and MJ's stories have been intertwined

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:53.180
for years.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:58.440
She's first mentioned way, way back in 1964's
Amazing Spider-Man #15.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.510
But it took two years before fans (and Peter)
finally got a look at her face, which first

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:07.979
showed up in Amazing Spider-Man #42 in 1966.

00:01:07.979 --> 00:01:12.130
As the years went on, MJ became Peter's on-again,
off-again girlfriend.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:15.969
In the '80s, she took their relationship to
the next level when she revealed that she'd

00:01:15.969 --> 00:01:19.920
figured out his secret identity in Amazing
Spider-Man #257.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:25.320
Meanwhile, there were more than a few hints
in Homecoming that indicated MJ has feelings

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.799
for Peter — and may even know his secret…

00:01:27.799 --> 00:01:29.420
"Where are you going?

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:30.960
What are you hiding…

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:31.960
Peter?"

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:37.039
Eventually, Peter and Mary Jane married in
1987's Amazing Spider-Man Annual #21.

00:01:37.039 --> 00:01:40.530
Twenty years later, in the critically divisive
One More Day storyline, Peter literally sold

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:45.170
his marriage to Mary Jane to the devil to
keep his old, decrepit Aunt May from croaking

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:47.170
after she was shot by bad guys.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:51.939
Needless to say, this new movie MJ has a lot
of weird times to look forward to.

00:01:51.939 --> 00:01:53.119
Ned

00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:56.649
Ned Leeds in the comics doesn't much resemble
the Ned we got in Homecoming.

00:01:56.649 --> 00:02:02.020
Leeds started out as a Daily Bugle reporter
back in the 1960s, and soon became a romantic

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:03.459
rival for Peter.

00:02:03.459 --> 00:02:07.759
Both of the guys were interested in the same
girl — Betty Brant — and eventually Peter

00:02:07.759 --> 00:02:10.820
backed off, while Ned and Betty wound up getting
married.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:15.410
But the union was not meant to last, because
the villain known as Hobgoblin brainwashed

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:20.080
Ned into becoming another Hobgoblin — all
so he could have a fall-guy to take the blame

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.070
if the cops got too close.

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:27.090
Eventually, Leeds would get pathetically murdered
in a hotel room while dressed up as Hobgoblin.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.840
Poor, dead Ned.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:29.970
But that's not all!

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:34.050
Many fans have pointed out that the Ned we
get in the movie bears a strong resemblance

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:38.640
to Ganke Lee, the best friend of Ultimate
Spider-Man, Miles Morales.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.110
It's not entirely clear why the movie's producers
decided to smoosh these two characters together

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:44.230
into one.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:48.120
But if he turns out to have more in common
with his namesake than his lookalike, our

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:51.110
friend Ned better watch out for brainwashing
machines.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:52.110
Seriously.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.100
Aaron Davis

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:58.820
On the subject of Ultimate versions of characters,
Donald Glover plays Aaron Davis, who gets

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:02.850
mixed up with Adrian Toomes and his gang of
super-weapon salvagers.

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:07.210
In the comics, that name belongs to the Prowler,
who happens to also be the uncle of Miles

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:08.210
Morales.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:13.320
In fact, it's because of the Prowler's extra-legal
shenanigans that Miles gets his spidery powers.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:17.721
In Homecoming, Davis hears about climbing
gear on offer from the Vulture's gang, and

00:03:17.721 --> 00:03:21.340
also mentions later that he's got a nephew
in the city he cares about.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:25.480
So don't be surprised to see Davis make a
comeback in a sequel — right along with

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:28.730
the movie version of fan-favorite Miles Morales.

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:29.730
Mac Gargan

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:34.410
Spider-Man's had a lot of villains over the
years, and few have been as persistent as

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:35.680
Mac Gargan.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:39.890
Originally, Mac assumed the identity of the
costumed bad guy the Scorpion.

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:44.530
Fun fact: the Scorpion was the result of mad
science gone wrong, all bankrolled by The

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:47.850
Daily Bugle publisher J. Jonah Jameson.

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:52.461
Because Mac got stuck in the Scorpion suit,
he's long held a grudge against Jameson, as

00:03:52.461 --> 00:03:54.040
well as Spider-Man.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:58.251
Years later, he bonded with the alien symbiote
— the one that tried to eat Peter Parker

00:03:58.251 --> 00:04:01.050
back in the '80s — to become the new Venom.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:05.330
The partnership didn't last too long, and
eventually ol' Mac went back to being the

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:06.520
Scorpion.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:10.230
Considering he put in an appearance during
the Ferry scene and during the mid-credits

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:14.740
scene in the prison, not to mention his scorpion
tattoo, chances seem good that he'll be back

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:18.639
with a much larger role to play in a Spider-sequel.

00:04:18.639 --> 00:04:19.680
Shocker

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:24.680
In Homecoming, we get not one, but two incarnations
of classic Spidey villain the Shocker.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:29.020
The first one bites the big one when he crosses
the Vulture, so Herman Schultz takes on the

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:31.750
vibro-knuckles to become the second Shocker.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:35.420
Comic fans know that Herman Schultz is the
Shocker's real name from the comics.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:39.270
But while he's a mainstay of Spider-Man's
rogues gallery, Shocker's never really been

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:40.600
an A-list villain.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:44.510
That's just kinda what happens when your costume
consists of a brown V-neck that goes down

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:47.680
to your crotch and a yellow quilt on your
face.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:49.000
The Tinkerer

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:54.010
Adrian Toomes' mechanically inclined hench-guy
is Phineas Mason — better known in the comics

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:56.160
by his villainous alter ego, The Tinkerer.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.160
Okay, as far as intimidating super-villain
names go, the Tinkerer isn't that good.

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:03.669
But it's still better than Paste Pot Pete,
right?

00:05:03.669 --> 00:05:05.010
And Stilt-Man.

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:06.639
Everyone's better than Stilt Man.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:11.940
The Tinkerer first appeared all the way back
in 1963's Amazing Spider-Man #2 — which

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.900
also happened to be the first appearance of
the Vulture.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:19.330
Since that time, he's hung around in the background
of the Marvel Universe, inventing gadgets

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:22.070
and upgrading devices for plenty of bad guys.

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:27.130
That includes dudes like Big Wheel, the Grizzly,
and the aforementioned Stilt Man.

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:30.250
Because, seriously, you can never have too
much Stilt Man.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:31.570
Thanks for watching!

00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:34.540
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:36.490
Plus check out all this other cool stuff we
know you'll love too.

